# Files excluded from Semgrep scanning because every finding Semgrep ever
# raises on them is a confirmed false positive / accepted-risk finding --
# see samples/genie/c++/Service/ci-local/NOTES.md for the full root-cause
# analysis and per-file rationale.
#
# Why a whole-file exclude instead of an inline `# nosemgrep` /
# `// nosemgrep` comment: the external gate script this repo's CI pipeline
# runs (qualcomm/qcom-reusable-workflows' check_sarif_error.py) reads every
# SARIF `result` and falls back to the *rule's* default severity whenever
# `result.level` isn't a string -- which is exactly the case for a
# nosemgrep-suppressed finding (Semgrep sets `level` to null/omitted and
# adds a `suppressions` field instead, which that script never reads). So
# a suppressed finding still counts as an "error-severity finding" to that
# gate and still fails the build, no matter how the suppression comment is
# written. A `.semgrepignore` entry keeps the file out of the SARIF
# `results` array entirely, which is the only lever available from this
# repo's side (the gate script lives in an external repo we don't control).

